<section class="tq-hero"><div class="tq-x tq-hero-g"><div><p class="tq-tag">Watches, explained properly</p><h1>Every hand on the dial is there for a reason.</h1><p class="tq-lede">Timique explains what watches can do beyond telling the time, and how to build and enjoy a small collection. Use the tools to set a second time zone, read a tachymeter or plan your rotation.</p><p class="tq-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Try the tools</a><a class="tq-more" href="watch-complications-guide.html">What is a complication?</a></p></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1788791084863-72bc3bc3076d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=720&h=620&q=75" alt="brown chronograph watch with a leather strap" width="720" height="620" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tq-quick"><div class="tq-x tq-quick-g"><a href="#tools"><b>01</b><h3>Second time zone</h3><p>Find the time away and where the GMT hand should point.</p></a><a href="#tools"><b>02</b><h3>Tachymeter</h3><p>Turn elapsed seconds into speed or an hourly rate.</p></a><a href="#tools"><b>03</b><h3>Rotation</h3><p>Plan which watch to wear so none sits forgotten.</p></a></div></section>

<section class="tq-s"><div class="tq-x tq-gl"><div><p class="tq-tag">The vocabulary</p><h2>Six complications you will meet</h2><dl class="tq-terms"><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>A numbered disc showing the day of the month through a window.</dd></div><div><dt>Chronograph</dt><dd>A stopwatch built into the watch, started and stopped by pushers.</dd></div><div><dt>GMT</dt><dd>A fourth hand on a 24-hour scale for a second time zone.</dd></div><div><dt>Moonphase</dt><dd>A disc showing the current phase of the moon.</dd></div><div><dt>Power reserve</dt><dd>A gauge showing how much running time is left in the spring.</dd></div><div><dt>Day-date</dt><dd>The weekday spelled out alongside the date.</dd></div></dl></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793127251083-bdafea42ac6c?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=640&q=75" alt="macro view of a black watch face with white numerals" width="560" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tq-s tq-navy" id="tools"><div class="tq-x tq-tools"><div class="tq-tools-i"><p class="tq-tag">Watch tools</p><h2>Three calculators for the wrist</h2><p>Each one answers a question that owners of GMT watches, chronographs and small collections ask sooner or later. They work in your browser and store nothing.</p></div><div class="tq-tools-l">
<div class="tq-card"><h3>Second Time Zone Finder</h3><div class="tq-f"><label for="tz-t">Time at home<input id="tz-t" type="time" value="09:30"></label><label for="tz-h">Home is UTC<select id="tz-h"></select></label><label for="tz-a">Away is UTC<select id="tz-a"></select></label></div><div class="tq-o" id="tz-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tq-card"><h3>Tachymeter Calculator</h3><div class="tq-f"><label for="tc-s">Seconds elapsed<input id="tc-s" type="number" min="7.2" max="600" step="0.1" value="45" inputmode="decimal"></label><label for="tc-u">Measured over<select id="tc-u"><option value="km">1 kilometre</option><option value="mi">1 mile</option><option value="task">1 task or item</option></select></label></div><div class="tq-o" id="tc-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tq-card"><h3>Rotation Planner</h3><div class="tq-f"><label for="rp-n">Watches you wear<input id="rp-n" type="number" min="1" max="20" value="4" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rp-a">Of which mechanical<input id="rp-a" type="number" min="0" max="20" value="3" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="rp-p">Typical power reserve<select id="rp-p"><option value="40">About 40 hours</option><option value="70">About 70 hours</option><option value="120">Five days or more</option></select></label></div><div class="tq-o" id="rp-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="tq-s"><div class="tq-x tq-chrono"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1649357585015-179ed98f513d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=620&q=75" alt="close-up of a person wearing a watch" width="600" height="620" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="tq-tag">In practice</p><h2>How to use a chronograph</h2><ol class="tq-steps"><li><b>Start.</b> Press the top pusher. The long central seconds hand begins to sweep.</li><li><b>Stop.</b> Press the top pusher again. Read the seconds from the central hand and the minutes from the small counter.</li><li><b>Resume if needed.</b> A third press of the top pusher carries on timing from where you stopped.</li><li><b>Reset.</b> Press the bottom pusher, only when the chronograph is stopped, to return every hand to zero.</li></ol><p class="tq-note">The small seconds hand that never stops belongs to the time, not the stopwatch.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="tq-s tq-grey"><div class="tq-x"><p class="tq-tag">Compare</p><h2>Which complication earns its place?</h2><div class="tblw"><table class="tq-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Complication</th><th>Useful when</th><th>Upkeep</th><th>Adds thickness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Date</td><td>Every day</td><td>Correct five times a year</td><td>Hardly</td></tr><tr><td>Chronograph</td><td>Cooking, parking, exercise</td><td>More parts to service</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>GMT</td><td>Travel, family abroad</td><td>None</td><td>A little</td></tr><tr><td>Moonphase</td><td>For the pleasure of it</td><td>Reset if the watch stops</td><td>A little</td></tr><tr><td>Power reserve</td><td>Hand-wound watches</td><td>None</td><td>Hardly</td></tr><tr><td>Day-date</td><td>Office life</td><td>Reset if the watch stops</td><td>Hardly</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="tq-s"><div class="tq-x"><p class="tq-tag">Read more</p><h2>Two guides</h2><div class="tq-gds"><a href="watch-complications-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1734209487725-a5472ccb044b?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=280&q=75" alt="black and white photograph of a watch" width="360" height="280" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide A</span><h3>The Complications Guide</h3><p>Date, chronograph, GMT, moonphase and tachymeter: how each works and how to set it without harm.</p></div></a><a href="watch-collection-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1694683865764-3b59cb4965b5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=360&h=280&q=75" alt="close-up of a watch on a table" width="360" height="280" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide B</span><h3>The Collecting Guide</h3><p>The three-watch idea, storing and rotating what you own, and keeping simple records.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="tq-s tq-grey"><div class="tq-x tq-three"><div><p class="tq-tag">A simple idea</p><h2>The three-watch collection</h2><div class="tq-trio"><article><b>1</b><h3>The everyday</h3><p>Steel, 38 to 40 mm, on a bracelet. Goes with everything and asks for nothing.</p></article><article><b>2</b><h3>The tough one</h3><p>Rated to 100 m or more, legible in the dark, on rubber or fabric. For water, sport and holidays.</p></article><article><b>3</b><h3>The dress watch</h3><p>Slim, plain dial, leather strap. Slides under a cuff on the few days a year that need it.</p></article></div></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1785433084117-7b292a002d90?auto=format&fit=crop&w=520&h=640&q=75" alt="three watches, a keyboard and a wallet on a desk" width="520" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tq-s"><div class="tq-x tq-fq"><div><p class="tq-tag">FAQ</p><h2>Short answers</h2></div><div class="tq-faq"><details open><summary>What is a watch complication?</summary><p>Any function beyond showing hours, minutes and seconds. A date window, a stopwatch, a second time zone and a moonphase display are all complications.</p></details><details><summary>How does a GMT watch show two time zones?</summary><p>It has an extra hand that circles the dial once every 24 hours and points to a 24-hour scale. The normal hands show local time, and the extra hand shows the time at home or in any zone you choose.</p></details><details><summary>What is a tachymeter used for?</summary><p>The scale around a chronograph&rsquo;s bezel converts elapsed seconds into a rate per hour. Time how long it takes to travel one kilometre or one mile, and the seconds hand points to your average speed.</p></details><details><summary>How many watches make a sensible collection?</summary><p>Three covers almost every situation: one everyday watch, one that can get wet and knocked, and one slim watch for formal clothes. Beyond that, collect what you enjoy wearing.</p></details><details><summary>Do automatic watches need a watch winder?</summary><p>No. A winder is a convenience for watches with calendars that are tedious to reset. A simple automatic is perfectly happy being left to stop and then wound and set when you next wear it.</p></details><details><summary>Should I leave the chronograph running all the time?</summary><p>It does no immediate harm, but it adds wear and shortens the power reserve on a mechanical watch. Run it when you are timing something and stop it afterwards.</p></details></div></div></section>

